1. A File System is the way Linux stores, organizes, and manages files and directories.

In Linux, everything starts from one top-level directory called the Root Directory (/).

2. Real-Life Example

Imagine a large office building.

The building = Root Directory (/)
Each floor = A directory (like /home, /etc, /var)
Each room = A subdirectory
Each file cabinet = Files

Everything is organized inside the building.

Similarly, in Linux, everything is organized under the Root Directory (/).

3. Linux File System Structure

                    /
          (Root Directory)
                 │
 ├── /bin
 ├── /boot
 ├── /dev
 ├── /etc
 ├── /home
 ├── /lib
 ├── /media
 ├── /mnt
 ├── /opt
 ├── /proc
 ├── /root
 ├── /run
 ├── /sbin
 ├── /srv
 ├── /sys
 ├── /tmp
 ├── /usr
 └── /var

4. Let's understand each subdirectory in detail

a)/ (Root Directory)

This is the top-most directory.

Every file and directory in Linux starts from here.

Example:

/home
/etc
/var

All these directories are inside /.

Do not confuse / (root directory) with /root (root user's home directory).

b)/home

Stores home directories of normal users.

Example

/home/bilal
/home/john

Each user gets a personal workspace.

Example

cd /home/bilal

c)/root

This is the home directory of the root (administrator) user.

Example

/root

Root user files are stored here.

Normal users usually cannot access this directory.

d)/bin (Binary)

Contains essential user commands required for booting and basic system operation.

Examples

ls
cp
mv
cat
pwd
rm

Example

ls /bin

On many modern Linux distributions, /bin is a symbolic link to /usr/bin, but the purpose remains the same.

e)/sbin (System Binary)

Contains essential system administration commands.

Examples

fsck
reboot
shutdown

Example

ls /sbin

On many modern systems, /sbin is also linked into /usr/sbin.

f)/etc

Contains system configuration files.

Examples

/etc/passwd
/etc/group
/etc/hosts
/etc/fstab

System administrators frequently work here.

Example

cat /etc/hosts

g)/boot

Contains files required to boot the Linux operating system.

Examples

Linux Kernel
GRUB configuration
Initial RAM disk (initramfs)

Example

ls /boot

h)/dev (Devices)

Linux treats hardware devices as files.

This directory contains device files.

Examples

/dev/sda
/dev/null
/dev/tty
/dev/random

i)/proc

A virtual filesystem that provides information about:

Running processes
CPU
Memory
Kernel

Example

cat /proc/cpuinfo

or

cat /proc/meminfo

j)/sys

Another virtual filesystem.

Contains information about:

Hardware devices
Drivers
Kernel settings

Example

ls /sys

k)/var (Variable Data)

Stores data that changes frequently.

Examples

Log files
Mail
Cache
Print spool files

Important directory

/var/log

Example

ls /var/log

l)/tmp (Temporary Files)

Stores temporary files.

Applications use this directory for temporary data.

Example

touch /tmp/test.txt

Many systems automatically clean old files from /tmp.

m)/opt (Optional Software)

Used to install third-party applications.

Examples

/opt/google
/opt/oracle

n)/media

Used for automatically mounted removable devices.

Examples

USB drives
DVD/CD

Example

/media/bilal/USB

o)/mnt

Used for temporary manual mounts.

Example

sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt

After mounting

/mnt

contains the contents of the device.

p)/srv

Stores data served by system services.

Examples

Web server files
FTP server data

Example

/srv/www

q)/run

Stores runtime information created after the system boots.

Examples

Process IDs (PID files)
Socket files
Runtime service data

Contents are cleared after reboot.
